{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers","id":262225,"title":"Irem and the Nameless Empire Discussion [SPOILERS]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-21T09:38:19-07:00","id":262225,"title":"Irem and the Nameless Empire Discussion [SPOILERS]","content":"Share your thoughts on the Nameless Empire!<br /> <br /> Going from bits scattered around the core, its borders extended from Asia Minor in the north, to Mesopotamia in the East, North Africa in the West, and down into Sudan and Ethopia in the South. That's big. <br /> It ended around 6,000 years ago. More specifically, around 3,832 B.C., the rough date of the start of the Sothic Turns. Interestingly enough, this was also around the time the 5.9 Kiloyear Event occured. The 5.9 Kiloyear Event was an intense period of aridification and desertification in North Africa. More specifically in the land that is now the Sahara Desert.<br /> <br /> They weren't the only magically led civilization of their day. Ubar took some effort for even the Shan'iatu to bring down.<br /> <br /> While it seems like most of their conquests just shipped resources back to Irem and didn't really get much out of the deal, the fact that there are still loads of Iremite relics lying around seems to indicate that everything wasn't <i>entirely</i> centralized. Also the fact that the Arisen even had tombs at all that weren't swallowed up when the city took the elevator to Duat (although that may be a side effect of the first or second turns, we still don't really know how the first resurrection went down).<br /> <br /> The early bits in the player's side of the book say that Irem brought agriculture and civilization to the savage proto-Iremites and their conquests. I'm pretty sure that's mostly propaganda. While yes, they probably did teach early civilizations lots of things about math and architecture and sacrificing nations to elder gods, the fact that the Shan'iatu were imperialistic as all get out really colors that view.<br /> <br /> Anyone else notice or come up with anything interesting regarding Irem?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3738-illuminated-minutia","name":"Illuminated Minutia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3738_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-21T10:26:55-07:00","id":262252,"title":"","content":"I've always kinda rejected a lot of what the book says about canon Irem. For one, I consider the various Guilds to not be connected at all to Iremite Guilds, but rather created in the first Sothic turn as Mummies struggled with just tiny glimpses of Irem, way less than what the book says they had, to recreate meaning. My idea of Irem is also both larger and vaguer. The Iremite empire covered the whole mediterranean, from Spain and southern France, including Italy, the Balkan coast, Greece, all of Anatolia, the Crimea, as far east as the Indus, as far south as the source of the Nile and even including the Niger river valley in Western Africa. I also prefer to think of &quot;Irem&quot; not being known to be centered in the Nile river valley, but rather could have been situated anywhere within that Empire. So instead of being an Egypt-o-centric empire, it becomes a Near-East empire, where the Mummies could have been originally from the Indus or Caucasian from the Crimea or Iberian or proto-Greek or whatever. <br /> I have Mummies remember much less of their past, so their identity isn't based on who they were in Irem, but rather is based on bits and pieces of their Descents that most resonated with them and that were defining moments in their life, and their face reflects that. A Mummy who helped redirect the Yangtze river to free an ancient artifact may look very Chinese and FEEL Chinese because of the intense connection they made with the Chinese people in that monumental task.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-21T11:02:13-07:00","id":262299,"title":"","content":"Personally, the most interesting thing left vague about Irem as an Empire was how long it lasted? <br /> <br /> I mean what do we really know about Irem's timeline? <br /> <br /> We know a few things about the time scale involved from the corebook. That it took the Shan'Iatu about 100 years to get the &quot;first tribes&quot; they subjugated (which presumably only took a hand full of years) ready to march out and wage war and form the Empire in the first place. <br /> <br /> We know that around 3,832BC they started performing the Rite of Return during &quot;Sothic Turn 0&quot;. How long it took them to perform all those rites is not known, but the fact that Guildhalls talks about certain Mummy's remembering it going on for a while (First of the Arisen being a title given to one of the Scribe NPC's for example). Likewise we know they experimented for quite some time beforehand during Irems warring years on slaves and captured prisoners to try and get the Rite of Return to work properly (resulting in the Shuankshen). <br /> <br /> From the tid-bits we have about the Deceived we know that the Sixth Guild was destroyed before any Rites of Return started to happen. So that suggests their was a period while the Empire was established proper that no Rites of Return were going on and the Guilds were important enough to be active, fully formed social constructs. <br /> <br /> The enactment of the Shan'Iatu's final plan for Irem must have happened not long after they finished off performing the various Rites of Return on their servants. <br /> <br /> As stands you could say that Irem stood for little over 100 years (say 120ish) or you could argue for a much longer period (maybe as much as 1000 years, though there is no real evidence at current for such a long time-frame) of time had passed between the &quot;first tribes&quot; being conjured and the Shan'Iatu's final plan coming to fruition. <br /> <br /> Personally, in the games I have run I had Irem last about 500 years from inception to disappearance from around 4,300BC to 3,800BC. Making its end about 800 years before the foundation of Dynastic Ancient Egypt. This also makes Irem comparable to early Sumerian/Akkadian settlement (of which Ubar was one) but keeps it early enough that the Wheel is pretty contemporary invention and most places in the world think the plough is a cutting edge farming invention (generally Neolithic cultures around the world were getting into proper farm animal domestication and cultivating rice and sugar cane between 5000-4000BC). So this keeps Irem as &quot;the most advanced&quot; civilization of the time, while still giving them some other societies to interact with as is mentioned in the fluff.<br /> <br /> I expect Book of the Deceived to be a bit more exact with the timeline of Irem and answer a lot of these questions to be fair. So I suspect my person picture of the Empire and how long it lasted will change quite a bit after that book. <br /> <br /> Geographically I keep it about as constrained as cannon has it, mainly North/Central African (Southern boarder in line with Modern day Niger/Sudan/Chad, Western boarder at Algeria/Mali) with a bit of the Middle East to the East. In my last game I also included Turkey and Saudi Arabia in the Empire. Normally I stop North-Easterly boarder as being in Syria, Iraq and the Red Sea. <br /> <br /> I don't see the Empire as the largest the world has ever seen, that's never really mention in the fluff and I'm sure its something the Arisen would make a bigger deal out of if it were the case. Considering a fair bit of the fluff is about how the Empire was more technologically advanced (they had bronze!), magically powerful and well...first! Also, just due to world population at the time, controlling much more land then that would have been a really pain, especially once the Sahara started getting larger.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-21T20:31:22-07:00","id":262620,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=262252#post262252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...I consider the various Guilds to not be connected at all to Iremite Guilds, but rather created in the first Sothic turn as Mummies struggled with just tiny glimpses of Irem, way less than what the book says they had, to recreate meaning....their identity isn't based on who they were in Irem, but rather is based on bits and pieces of their Descents that most resonated with them and that were defining moments in their life, and their face reflects that. A Mummy who helped redirect the Yangtze river to free an ancient artifact may look very Chinese and FEEL Chinese because of the intense connection they made with the Chinese people in that monumental task.</div> </div> </div> </div>If not the books, then definitely some of the writers had basically this <i>is</i> how it is - Arisen who have been in China for thousands of years identify themselves as Chinese more than proto-Egyptian. Of course this happens more at lower Memory, but even higher Memory Arisen might face an internal identity crisis as, while they remember more of their time in the Empire, they've spent their physical and emotional un-lives in China.<br /> <br /> Again, the same was implied by writers, too, that the Guilds as the Arisen have structured them didn't show up until their first awakening in the Sothic turn. Thousands of Mummies wake up, realize their precious Empire is gone, and establish the Guilds to mimic how their mortal lives were spent in guilds during the Empire. They're certainly not <i>exactly</i> the same as they were during Irem, but the Arisen tried/do their best.<br /> <br /> I'm glad to know I'm not the only one that caught the bit about Ubar, too. I'm looking forward to how that might come into play, probably in Sothis Ascends?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-21T20:51:24-07:00","id":262631,"title":"","content":"It's also notable that a starting Mummy remembers nothing about their own life in Irem, merely that they did live in Irem. The bits and drabs they can recall are impossible to separate from what others have told them about Irem, and so it's all a bit unreliable.<br /> <br /> Personally, I've not done anything special with Irem in my games. It's largely confined to North-East Africa, no bigger than what's described in the lore. Absolutely no Mummies with Caucasian features at all, unless they're Twice-Arisen. I'm not really interested in the scope of the empire, but rather who the characters were back then and how they lived. The day-to-day experience is what interests me, because that's the stuff I'm going to be bringing up in flashbacks.<br /> <br /> With that in mind, one of my favourite tidbits about Irem comes from Cursed Necropolis D.C. Seb-Hetchet, speaking about what he remembers of Irem, says, &quot;Before I went to die, I walked the streets without seeing slaves. I saw them emptied of the people. What did our leaders <i>do</i>?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-22T02:52:59-07:00","id":262734,"title":"","content":"In terms of actual play application: I enjoy using Arisen characters (both as PCs and NPCs) who all have some sort of strong opinion on the Nameless Empire and their origins. It's the lost origin of all mummies, the only place where they ever felt home, and it's forever gone, right?<br /> <br /> Sample stances on the Nameless Empire:<br /> <br /> .) The Nameless Empire was the only real empire ever to exist. None others have been sanctioned by the gods. In time, they will all be destroyed. Exploiting them and furthering their destruction is no sin.<br /> <br /> .) What has fallen must rise again. We shall rebuild Irem, greater than it ever was, and construct a new empire. Surely you see that all empires are forged in blood, yes?<br /> <br /> .) Things are now better than ever. Why should we go back to a past that can't ever come back?<br /> <br /> Etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-22T06:23:15-07:00","id":262807,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Illuminated Minutia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers#post262225\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They weren't the only magically led civilization of their day. Ubar took some effort for even the Shan'iatu to bring down.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. My own view of the Shan'iatu is that they weren't mystical powerhouses, even if mummies sometimes think they must have been. What made them remarkable was the level to which they industrialised their magic, and integrated it into their technology and cultural systems.<br /> <br /> I do like the idea of Irem being created out of the kind of whole cloth suggested in the core book. This is actually because I kind of like that as a parallel to the modern world - mummies come from a place where technology was making quantum leaps (bronze and civil engineering), where a communications revolution was taking place (writing), and where concepts that hadn't existed a mere century earlier were now part of daily life. The modern world feels like home on a level that could never have been predicted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also the fact that the Arisen even had tombs at all that weren't swallowed up when the city took the elevator to Duat (although that may be a side effect of the first or second turns, we still don't really know how the first resurrection went down). </div> </div> </div>Whatever the Shan'iatu truly had in mind for the mummies, it seems unlikely to be a purpose that would be better served by <i>not</i> setting up Tombs for them. And if there's one thing about the Shan'iatu we can be sure of, it's that they were meticulous planners.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The early bits in the player's side of the book say that Irem brought agriculture and civilization to the savage proto-Iremites and their conquests. I'm pretty sure that's mostly propaganda. While yes, they probably did teach early civilizations lots of things about math and architecture and sacrificing nations to elder gods, the fact that the Shan'iatu were imperialistic as all get out really colors that view. </div> </div> </div>I think they might have been the ones to teach all that stuff to the proto-Iremites; but I agree they weren't the only - or even necessarily the first.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Christian A</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=262734#post262734\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In terms of actual play application: I enjoy using Arisen characters (both as PCs and NPCs) who all have some sort of strong opinion on the Nameless Empire and their origins. It's the lost origin of all mummies, the only place where they ever felt home, and it's forever gone, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>.) We <i>are</i> Irem. The Empire was dependent on no single one of its physical structures, or citizens, or slaves, or even the Shan'iatu themselves. Instead, it's a legacy and a meaning, and that's carried inside us even now.<br /> <br /> While I'm not satisfied with the dodge for mummies identifying with other cultures rather than Irem. (Or to expound, I'm not satisfied with it as an answer to the &quot;why no mummies from other cultures&quot; question - it pretty much amounts to &quot;well, your character can always be delusional&quot; - especially in a game that deliberately distinguishes itself as having a secret history that remains true whatever individual mummies believe. However, the actual tone of the game is suited to a single origin, and think mummies of other cultures are better represented as another critter rather than hacking Curse's setting). But that aside, I can see it in terms of an actual decision by a mummy, or a judgment on Irem itself.<br /> <br /> .) Irem failed. It left a few relics, but not enough that anyone remembers it or its legacy. Even us. I choose to align my identity with this other culture because this other culture has marked the world to the greater extent: it's earned my loyalty where Irem merely demanded it.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-22T18:50:38-07:00","id":263304,"title":"","content":"Just some musings on the Shan'iatu and the purpose of Irem.<br /> <br /> I was googling WoD stuff earlier and found that old interview with CAS. It seems like back when that interview happened, everyone was focusing on the Judges =\\= Shan'iatu.<br /> <br /> I saw these two questions.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <span style=\"color:#0F2105\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\"><i><u>2. Does Anpu have a particular plan for the Arisen?</u></i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">He certainly does play a central and unique role in their unlives, doesn\u2019t he. Whether that role constitutes a \u201cplan\u201d is a different question, I think; and another question still might be, \u201cIf it does constitute a \u2018plan,\u2019 is it really Anpu\u2019s plan?\u201d You\u2019re right to ask the question, though. The workings of Fate are a core part of the game for a reason, after all.</span></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#0F2105\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\"><i><u>3. Why did the Shan\u2019iatu bargain with Ammut in particular for power?</u></i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">This question is interesting in thatit pre-supposes that the Shan\u2019iatu had a choice.</span></span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> And then I remembered a few bits from the Corebook.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">(P. 225)<br /> Minor djed are indestructible to ordinary forces, and remain solid against spiritual beings such as ghosts and astral bodies (beings said to exist in a state of \u201cTwilight\u201d), as well as entities passing through spirit worlds that lay parallel to the physical.<br /> <br /> (P. 174)<br /> What the Arisen call the Neter-Khertet is known to many of its spectral inhabitants as \u201cliving in Twilight.\u201d<br /> <br /> (P. 171)<br /> When the Arisen fall from the living world into Neter-Khertet, it is not the face of the Beautiful One that greets them, nor even the dread visages of their precious Judges.<br /> ...<br /> Anpu appears most often when a mummy is killed by his enemies and must return to finish the job, but technically, he can appear to them any time they\u2019re physically inside or manifested in Twilight.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Were the Shan'iatu <i>running</i>?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3738-illuminated-minutia","name":"Illuminated Minutia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3738_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-22T18:56:22-07:00","id":263307,"title":"","content":"Oh man, that is a beautiful theory. Anpu was always one of the things that stuck out the most for me in the core book. Anything that lends a bit more heft to his presence in the game is fantastic.<br /> <br /> Although, I'm really hoping a later book will take a more expanded look at Apotheosis and the role Sutek may play in it. If Anpu isn't behind it ultimately, as suggested by &quot;is it really Anpu's plan?&quot; then the same force could be driving all of Irem's gods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-23T07:01:48-07:00","id":263739,"title":"","content":"I don't really consider Anpu cruel or really malicious to the Arisen or Shan'iatu. I, however, subscribe to the theory that the Shan'iatu are <b>not</b> the Judges. So at best I see Anpu being resentful to the one who took his position, but not really bitter in his actions - maybe a cool, calm collection since he Knows that one day he'll have his place back. Maybe the Arisen will/are help him? There's certainly precedent for cosmic forces using Mummies as their tools.<br /> <br /> But I never considered any of that until just now. Anpu is mysterious enough as is, being a silent watcher in a realm most Arisen only see when they die mid-Descent","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-24T18:06:48-07:00","id":265041,"title":"","content":"I've generally seen him as a relatively passive, neutral character, yeah. The entire thing about how he steps aside for Azar, while not giving any validity or acknowledgement that his brother might have a greater claim, rather suits the character of a god who just isn't all that involved.<br /> <br /> I would really like to see a greater exploration of Sutek in a future supplement. I feel like he should play a key role in Apotheosis. His fingers are an integral part of the process, after all. Having a similar section on Sutek and the appearances he might make towards Mummies on the path to Apotheosis would be fantastic. Plus, Sutek sounds like an interesting brother. He both killed Azar, and helped him ascend to Heaven. And he's implied to be part of the gods who told Anpu to step aside from the throne of Duat as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-25T08:57:34-07:00","id":265504,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=265041#post265041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would really like to see a greater exploration of Sutek in a future supplement. I feel like he should play a key role in Apotheosis. His fingers are an integral part of the process, after all. Having a similar section on Sutek and the appearances he might make towards Mummies on the path to Apotheosis would be fantastic. Plus, Sutek sounds like an interesting brother. He both killed Azar, and helped him ascend to Heaven. And he's implied to be part of the gods who told Anpu to step aside from the throne of Duat as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would personally like to learn more about all the Nameless Gods, they seem interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#b22222\"><b>.</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3742-iceblade44","name":"Iceblade44","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"707"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T09:44:14-07:00","id":268307,"title":"","content":"With his tendency to show mummies visions from their lost memories and other such hints, I've gotten the impression that Anup's (the misspelling bugs me) plan involves steering most mummies gently toward Apotheosis. In my mind, he comes off neutral and passive simply because, as a god of death, he has both an extremely long perspective and a sense of his own inevitability.<br /> <br /> In my vision of Anup, he considers whatever the Shan'iatu did to be a temporary disruption (on a long enough timescale, 7 millenia is a blip). Eventually all their servitors (the mummies) will slip their chains like the Heretic has, the Judges and the Nameless City will starve in Duat, Azar will fall from his throne, and everything will go back to &quot;normal&quot;. From his extremely long view, even the slight nudging he's doing is crass impatience on his part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 09-29-2014, 12:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T10:04:23-07:00","id":268319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gabby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=268307#post268307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anup's (the misspelling bugs me)</div> </div> </div> </div>A cursory Googling shows that &quot;Anpu&quot; is the more common non-Greek rendering of the name from something like &quot;<i>inpw</i>,&quot; and the language has no written vowels to begin with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T10:23:44-07:00","id":268327,"title":"","content":"Judging by contemporary translations into other languages (including greek and akkadian) and the fact that the name itself is thought to be onomatopoeia, it's implied that that sign order was more of a cosmetic consideration. Which is a thing, the egyptians were fond of stacking long/small signs whenever they could get away with it, and the sign for w/u (a quail chick) is not easily stackable while the signs for &quot;n&quot; and &quot;p&quot; (the squiggle-line water symbol and the box) are. With the jackal determinative, the thinking was that everyone knew to pronounce it the right way anyway regardless of how they jumbled the signs. As transliteration into english tends to go for pronunciation rather than sign-order accuracy, anpu is probably wrong in the way that literally phonetically pronouncing a lot of english words is wrong.<br /> <br /> Also, while &quot;no written vowels&quot; is technically true, their idea of what a vowel is was a bit eccentric. From our definitions, signs like w, i, j, <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u02e4, <span style=\"font-size:12px\">and 3 (3 is totally a letter, I swear) are basically vowels. They're just not *all* the vowels.<br /> <br /> (Who has a countable number of thumbs and has been learning middle egyptian? This entity) </span></span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 09-29-2014, 01:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T10:32:48-07:00","id":268333,"title":"","content":"Thank you, that was edifying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T14:56:49-08:00","id":310578,"title":"","content":"Am I the only one seeing clear parallels between the Nameless Empire of MtC and Atlantis of MtAw? The very first thought I had after reading the history section was &quot;Huh! Sounds like either Atlantis colony or the refugies repeating the mistakes of the past.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3479-von-lyttenburgh","name":"von Lyttenburgh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3479_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T15:11:19-08:00","id":310587,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>von Lyttenburgh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310578#post310578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Am I the only one seeing clear parallels between the Nameless Empire of MtC and Atlantis of MtAw? The very first thought I had after reading the history section was &quot;Huh! Sounds like either Atlantis colony or the refugies repeating the mistakes of the past.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Atlantis, specially in the 2ed is a myth, a symbol of a lost civilization and the peak of human potential, and it exists out of time, is not a part of history. The Nameless Empire exists in the Wod timeline and is essently the foundation of human civilization.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T15:11:33-08:00","id":310588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>von Lyttenburgh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310578#post310578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Am I the only one seeing clear parallels between the Nameless Empire of MtC and Atlantis of MtAw?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, you're not.<br /> <br /> In a crossover happy game, Mages might think the Nameless Empire was Atlantis or one of the Atlantises. From what I understand, especially in the new edition, Atlantis is more about the Supernal representation of 'fallen empire' so...sure","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T15:13:09-08:00","id":310589,"title":"","content":"Keep in mind that all gamelines essently don't look at each other when writing their material. The only real exceptions is information about the Shadow and the Underworld I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T17:10:12-08:00","id":310737,"title":"","content":"Atlantis in 1e seems very similar to Irem, especially given the context of Book of the Deceived (which states that the Rite of Return may well have removed Irem from the timeline or at least displaced it somehow), while 2e seems to be treating Atlantis as more of a &quot;Platonic ideal of civilization&quot; that didn't quite as concretely ever exist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T20:33:28-08:00","id":310836,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310737#post310737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Atlantis in 1e seems very similar to Irem, especially given the context of Book of the Deceived (which states that the Rite of Return may well have removed Irem from the timeline or at least displaced it somehow), while 2e seems to be treating Atlantis as more of a &quot;Platonic ideal of civilization&quot; that didn't quite as concretely ever exist.</div> </div> </div> </div>2nd edition Mage's difference with Atlantis looks to be one more of presentation, rather than world metaphysics. As both 1st edition and second edition possess the idea of it as the reality where it existed being shattered and fragments of different versions of Atlantis still to be found here or there in the New World of Darkness. Where as Irem still looks to be a part of THIS interation of the NWoD. Though I am curious just how the Rite broke time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T20:50:44-08:00","id":310855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310836#post310836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where as Irem still looks to be a part of THIS interation of the NWoD. Though I am curious just how the Rite broke time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm inclined to look at it in terms of the tidbits scattered around Utterances like how <i>retroactively altering the course of a meteor</i> isn't even a top-tier effect or how the existence of the Black Stars indicates that the Rite <i>rearranged the firmament</i> as well as (or as part of?) ending the Age of the Scorpion. Something that kind of sunk in for me while reading Deceived was that Mummy's approach to metaphysics feels appropriately longest-view \u2014 the Arisen version of Fate fairly explicitly can't be gainsaid like its analogs implicitly may-or-may-not be, for instance, and regardless of any temporal meddling the notion of <i>cosmic inevitability</i> is easily visible in the line.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-12T21:45:49-08:00","id":310902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310855#post310855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm inclined to look at it in terms of the tidbits scattered around Utterances like how <i>retroactively altering the course of a meteor</i> isn't even a top-tier effect or how the existence of the Black Stars indicates that the Rite <i>rearranged the firmament</i> as well as (or as part of?) ending the Age of the Scorpion. Something that kind of sunk in for me while reading Deceived was that Mummy's approach to metaphysics feels appropriately longest-view \u2014 the Arisen version of Fate fairly explicitly can't be gainsaid like its analogs implicitly may-or-may-not be, for instance, and regardless of any temporal meddling the notion of <i>cosmic inevitability</i> is easily visible in the line.</div> </div> </div> </div>Beyond just the cool cosmic metaphysics and mythology the line has created. It's really making me work on my sorta kinda but not really unified NWoD cosmology, mostly just for crossover purposes. Book of the Deceived in particularly set up a lot of stuff if I ever do a Mummy/Mage crossover that will hopefully make any Mage PC's who bother to study certain things very very nervous around the Deathless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-13T03:26:21-08:00","id":311072,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310836#post310836\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 2nd edition Mage's difference with Atlantis looks to be one more of presentation, rather than world metaphysics. As both 1st edition and second edition possess the idea of it as the reality where it existed being shattered and fragments of different versions of Atlantis still to be found here or there in the New World of Darkness. Where as Irem still looks to be a part of THIS interation of the NWoD. Though I am curious just how the Rite broke time.</div> </div> </div> </div>It seens that the time-breaking thing was just the Judges of Death to have always existed I guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-13T04:40:59-08:00","id":311109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=310855#post310855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...and <b>regardless of any temporal meddling the notion of <i>cosmic inevitability</i> is easily visible in the line.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Man, you nailed it.<br /> <br /> It all kind of puts into perspective the claim that the Rite of Return was the single most powerful spell ever cast: it <i>broke</i> space-time in order to come to fruition, and then the Arisens' souls <i>still</i> managed to find loopholes through Utterance mastery, Memory, and Sothic awakenings (though <b>Book of the Deceived</b> has an interesting myth regarding that last bit).<br /> <br /> Of course in a <b>Mage</b> crossover one might have to assume it's at least equal to the Exarchs' Ascension - if they were even Mages that Ascended and if it was even a single spell. But what I'm just musing on is that the Rite had as much of a ramification in <b>Mummy</b>'s setting as the Ascension did in <b>Mage</b>'s","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-13T16:18:26-08:00","id":311715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=311109#post311109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Man, you nailed it.<br /> <br /> It all kind of puts into perspective the claim that the Rite of Return was the single most powerful spell ever cast: it <i>broke</i> space-time in order to come to fruition, and then the Arisens' souls <i>still</i> managed to find loopholes through Utterance mastery, Memory, and Sothic awakenings (though <b>Book of the Deceived</b> has an interesting myth regarding that last bit).<br /> <br /> Of course in a <b>Mage</b> crossover one might have to assume it's at least equal to the Exarchs' Ascension - if they were even Mages that Ascended and if it was even a single spell. But what I'm just musing on is that the Rite had as much of a ramification in <b>Mummy</b>'s setting as the Ascension did in <b>Mage</b>'s</div> </div> </div> </div>The biggest difference I guess is that it is possible in Mummy to know the truth of what happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T07:02:12-08:00","id":312236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Felipe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=311715#post311715\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The biggest difference I guess is that it is possible in Mummy to know the truth of what happened.</div> </div> </div> </div>Eh, maybe. Mummies can eventually remember everything that happened to them in the Nameless Empire, but they were never the Shan'iatu themselves, and so probably weren't privy to the deepest magical secrets of the Rite, its consequences, the truth of the Judges, etc. Even with respect to the Deceived, the temakh who ride their souls are broken fragments of what they once were, and their memories of what actually happened are...suspect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2497-brian888","name":"Brian888","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"168"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T07:17:44-08:00","id":312246,"title":"","content":"There's a key NPC in the chronicle at the end of Cursed Necropolis DC who does remember everything... Most he gets is that near the end Irem had considerably fewer people in it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T09:10:56-08:00","id":312309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brian888</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=312236#post312236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Eh, maybe. Mummies can eventually remember everything that happened to them in the Nameless Empire, but they were neveror the Shan'iatu themselves, and so probably weren't privy to the deepest magical secrets of the Rite, its consequences, the truth of the Judges, etc. Even with respect to the Deceived, the temakh who ride their souls are broken fragments of what they once were, and their memories of what actually happened are...suspect.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, unlike Atlantis(if it really existed) you just have one version of the truth, there have been so many rewrites of reality and time reguarding Atlantis that you can't ever find the real one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T09:25:52-08:00","id":312316,"title":"","content":"Reminds me of the exchange from the Principia Discordia:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> GP: Is Eris true?<br /> M2: Everything is true.<br /> GP: Even false things?<br /> M2: Even false things are true.<br /> GP: How can that be?<br /> M2: I don't know man, I didn't do it. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2497-brian888","name":"Brian888","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"168"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T09:27:55-08:00","id":312319,"title":"","content":"I'd wager to think that a mummy who has reached Apotheosis might well know a few things the Shan'iatu do not and cannot know. After all, the Shan'iatu have never been to Duat, while the Arisen spend most of their time in that place.<br /> <br /> Not that I, personally, really think it matters. Mummy, to me, empathically <i>isn't</i> a game about figuring out the Grand Truths about the Nameless Empire and the Shan'iatu and the Rite of Return. It's about memory and nostalgia and an exploration of old age and of social themes through the perspective of beings who've seen the best and worst sides of humanity throughout the entirety of history; all of which I find to be far more interesting than a mythical, fantastic past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T09:31:16-08:00","id":312323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Christian A</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/262225-irem-and-the-nameless-empire-discussion-spoilers?p=312319#post312319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd wager to think that a mummy who has reached Apotheosis might well know a few things the Shan'iatu do not and cannot know. After all, the Shan'iatu have never been to Duat, while the Arisen spend most of their time in that place.<br /> <br /> Not that I, personally, really think it matters. Mummy, to me, empathically <i>isn't</i> a game about figuring out the Grand Truths about the Nameless Empire and the Shan'iatu and the Rite of Return. It's about memory and nostalgia and an exploration of old age and of social themes through the perspective of beings who've seen the best and worst sides of humanity throughout the entirety of history; all of which I find to be far more interesting than a mythical, fantastic past.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's also about reclaiming oneself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-14T09:35:47-08:00","id":312327,"title":"","content":"Correct; that's my point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}